@@ -0,0 +1,353 @@
+# Restricted external-authority SharedTree prototype
+
+This source-aligned prototype starts at Fluid 2.111.0 release commit
+`7c3fc51d5d204a16299014921e93d522590a4af2`. It is **not** the user's
+`2.74.0-370705` Detach/Attach build. This revision additionally ports the public
+detached-root foundation at `1b49fc989e3d844cb7fc7c50d3df59d27c5ac702`, with
+2.111 conflict resolutions and higher-level reinsertion support described below.
+Normal mode retains its existing wire format and editing API behavior.
+
+## Opt in
+
+```ts
+import {
+    configuredSharedTreeAlpha,
+    getSharedTreeAuthority,
+} from "@fluidframework/tree/alpha";
+
+const AuthorityTree = configuredSharedTreeAlpha({
+    minVersionForCollab: "2.80.0",
+    externalAuthority: {
+        documentEpoch: "document-incarnation",
+        authorityId: "trusted-bridge",
+        authorityEpoch: "authority-incarnation",
+    },
+});
+```
+
+All clients must use this configuration. Authority trees deliberately use a
+different DDS type, ending in `/external-authority-v2`. This is a new experimental
+protocol, not wire-compatible with an unmodified tree. The Fluid ordering service
+is unchanged. IDs and epochs provide fencing; **they do not authenticate writers**.
+Every writer in this prototype is trusted and can access the controller.
+Version 2 adds an explicit authoring-base revision to authority advances and
+supports atomic prefix settlement plus correction. Its bootstrap and message
+envelopes also use version 2. Use fresh containers: r2/r3/r4 version-1 documents
+are not an in-place upgrade target, even if an application supplies matching labels.
+
+## Controller
+
+### r7 bounded public publication retries
+
+`publishAdvance` now supports identical same-process retries for both pure-prefix
+and correction publications. It snapshots the input properties on the first
+call and binds the batch ID to the exact owned capture, normalized prefix count,
+next checkpoint and owned draft identity/head. Equivalent snapshots or newly
+authored equivalent drafts are not identities.
+
+* Identical calls while the send is pending share one underlying submission and
+  report the same outcome. No result is cached before the local sequenced echo;
+  even an identical peer batch is not that local echo.
+* After the local echo, an applied publication retries as `duplicate`, while
+  stale remains `stale`. This also works before Promise callbacks have run.
+* Changed capture/prefix/checkpoint/draft reuse rejects without poisoning the
+  kernel or disturbing the first publication. Changed draft heads and unfinished
+  draft transactions reject, including on retries.
+* The capture and (for corrections) draft must remain undisposed for public
+  retries. Disposing the capture expires this API retry scope but does not cancel
+  its already submitted operation. Disposing the checkout rejects pending receipts.
+* At most 128 public publication records are retained per kernel/controller.
+  Dispose completed captures to release them; disposed captures with pending sends
+  retain their records until completion. There is also a 128 in-flight core limit.
+  Capacity rejection happens before sealing a new public draft.
+
+Controllers are canonicalized by underlying kernel, not the identity of an ITree
+facade. The core submission route also checks encoded identity outside the fatal
+breaker boundary and shares an identical in-flight receipt. True submission
+failures remain fatal and reject waiting receipts with the original error;
+the breaker is never cleared. A fatally broken checkout's existing disposal API
+can itself reject, so abandon that failed instance; this is not recovery support.
+
+Released public receipts cannot be reopened or substituted with a new capture.
+Previously sequenced batch IDs still cannot be repurposed: the existing wire
+batch ledger rejects that reuse. That ledger and authority history remain
+unbounded in this prototype; the 128-record limit is **not** a total memory or
+durable deduplication guarantee. Runtime restart, expired service history,
+cross-process receipts and replay preparation remain unsupported.
+
+No API signature, envelope version, ordering service or DDS type changes in r7.
+Runtime wire duplicate/reconnect paths remain separate from public API retry.
+
+The r7 all-M4 successor also makes capture disposal reentrant-safe. It first
+preflights every reference checkout without changing ownership, then marks the
+capture expired before disposing public drafts (which can invoke caller-owned
+revertible disposal callbacks). A callback that calls `capture.dispose()` again
+is a no-op. Transaction/change-event preflight failures leave the capture usable.
+Real teardown errors are not swallowed; internal A/S checkout cleanup runs in
+`finally`, and an already-started capture disposal remains expired.
+
+### r6 opt-in, capture-local node counterparts
+
+Ordinary `capture()` and authority wire protocol v2 are unchanged. The separate
+r6 successor adds `capture({ trackNodeReferences: true })`, returning a
+`SharedTreeAuthorityReferenceCapture`. It does not add fields to the document schema.
+
+```ts
+const cut = authority.capture({ trackNodeReferences: true });
+const prefixDraft = authority.createDraft(cut, { afterPrefix: k });
+const prefixView = prefixDraft.viewWith(configuration);
+const source = cut.reference(liveNode);
+if (source.kind === "tracked") {
+    const counterpart = cut.resolve(source.reference, prefixDraft);
+    if (counterpart.kind === "node") {
+        // counterpart.node belongs to prefixView's checkout, not the live checkout.
+        // Narrow its schema with Tree.is before making typed corrections.
+    }
+}
+```
+
+`reference(node)` accepts nodes from this controller's live view or an active
+unpublished draft belonging to this exact capture. It returns `tracked` with an
+opaque object reference, or `outsideCapture` for unhydrated nodes and births
+outside the captured sequenced history. A local unsequenced birth stays outside
+that old capture even after its Fluid acknowledgement. Correction-draft-born
+node -> eventual-live correspondence is deliberately unsupported; numeric native
+IDs, matching contents and schema identifiers cannot substitute for it.
+
+`resolve(reference, "live" | draft)` returns:
+
+* `node`, with the target checkout's wrapper and `inDocument` or `removed` status;
+* `absent`, when the node is absent from that historical projection, including
+  a prefix before its creation;
+* `unavailable` / `destroyed`, if a previously represented counterpart was destroyed.
+
+`removed` can also describe a descendant of a removed tree; existing reinsertion
+rules still admit only supported removed roots in the same checkout. Wrong
+captures/controllers/checkouts, forged references, sealed/disposed drafts,
+expired captures, missing/incompatible target views, unfinished transactions
+and transient change-event access are usage errors. Dispose the capture to
+release its anchors and all its reference-enabled draft checkouts.
+
+References are established once at an actual forest-clone boundary and then
+follow real delta visitors. The tracked S snapshot is projected from live state
+by rolling back L. Each tracked prefix draft forks S directly, before rolling
+back the excluded P suffix; it does not pass through A and recreate P-born nodes.
+Detached-root IDs are seeded from `DetachedFieldIndex`. Anchor IDs and wrapper
+caches are not shared between forests. Paths are used only as coordinates at the
+verified clone boundary, never for subsequent counterpart lookup or recovery.
+
+The opt-in eagerly anchors all stored nodes, including leaves and retained
+detached content. Defaults are `maxNodes: 10000`, `maxDrafts: 16` concurrently
+retained public reference drafts, and a fixed maximum node depth of 256.
+Optional positive safe-integer node/draft limits can be supplied. Exceeding a
+limit fails rather than truncating coverage. Space is O(nodes * retained views);
+this is not a production-scale lazy index. The node limit includes the temporary
+local snapshot, even though L-born nodes cannot yield public references.
+The enrollment limit does not cap subsequent live/draft edits, new ancestor
+paths, or the underlying retained history; there is no implicit eviction.
+
+References are valid only in this process/controller/capture lifetime. There is
+no token serialization, original-cut reopening, global identity, or durable
+native UUID sidecar contract. Existing authority-mode unbounded history retention
+is still required; holding an anchor alone does not pin repair data against
+destruction. All r5 summary/recovery and detached-content restrictions below remain.
+
+### Existing authority operations
+
+`getSharedTreeAuthority(tree)` exposes:
+
+* `capture()` — a consistent immutable approved checkpoint, ordered stable
+  `editIds`, `exportApproved()` and `exportSequenced()` snapshots. The latter is
+  **A+P**, never A+P+L. Snapshots are real checkout forks with history diffs applied
+  to their forests, schemas and removed-root indexes. They do not follow live edits.
+* `createDraft(capture)` — a private fork at **A**, edited with ordinary
+  `draft.viewWith(configuration)` and typed tree operations. It does not submit
+  ordinary Fluid operations.
+* `createDraft(capture, { afterPrefix: k })` — a private fork at **captured A plus
+  exactly the first k captured P revisions**. It excludes the remaining captured
+  P, later sequenced work, and L. Publishing this draft must use the same capture
+  object and `approvePrefix: k`.
+* `publishAdvance({ batchId, nextCheckpoint, capture, approvePrefix?, draft? })`
+  — publishes one authority message. The promise reports `"applied"`,
+  `"duplicate"` or `"stale"` only after normal sequenced self-echo.
+* `onStateChanged(listener)` — notification after sequenced processing; returns
+  an unsubscribe function.
+
+An unchanged `approvePrefix`, an A-based external draft, and a prefix-based
+correction are supported. A plain A-based draft combined with positive approval
+is still rejected: it was authored on the wrong history. Publishing seals the
+draft against new publications; r7 permits the exact original retry as described above. Complete any
+open draft transaction before publishing. Dispose drafts/captures when no longer
+needed. Snapshots use SharedTree's existing `VerboseTree` format; the authority
+controller does not perform package serialization or application-level merging.
+
+Example unchanged-prefix settlement:
+
+```ts
+const authority = getSharedTreeAuthority(tree);
+const cut = authority.capture();
+const outcome = await authority.publishAdvance({
+    batchId: "unique-external-batch",
+    nextCheckpoint: "unique-approved-checkpoint",
+    capture: cut,
+    approvePrefix: cut.editIds.length,
+});
+cut.dispose();
+```
+
+Example accepted-prefix settlement with an authoritative correction:
+
+```ts
+const cut = authority.capture();
+const k = cut.editIds.length; // Or an explicitly accepted leading prefix.
+const correction = authority.createDraft(cut, { afterPrefix: k });
+const correctedView = correction.viewWith(configuration);
+// Apply typed authoritative normalization to correctedView, whose base is A + P[0:k].
+const outcome = await authority.publishAdvance({
+    batchId: "unique-normalized-acceptance",
+    nextCheckpoint: "unique-approved-checkpoint",
+    capture: cut,
+    approvePrefix: k,
+    draft: correction,
+});
+correction.dispose();
+cut.dispose();
+```
+
+The accepted prefix is **not replaced**. Let B be its actual history head and C
+be the draft's newly authored corrections. The new approved history is B followed
+by C, with all accepted revisions preserved and fresh revision identities for C.
+The remaining P suffix rebases over C, then L rebases over that new sequenced
+history. The transition is emitted once through the checkout's rebase event.
+Even an empty/no-net correction does not settle any edits outside the selected
+prefix. No authoring context is reconstructed from `VerboseTree` or JSON.
+
+The wire advance carries B's stable revision as `authoringBase`, in addition to
+the approved checkpoint and exact prefix IDs. Sequenced processing validates
+that these resolve to the same actual history before appending corrections.
+Later ordinary edits do not invalidate a cut; a competing authority advance
+does. A capture of an earlier pre-attach A is also rejected if initialization
+changed A while the checkpoint remained `"initial"`. Malformed wire contexts
+fail closed through the existing SharedTree error/breaker mechanism.
+
+Attach/initialize the tree and approve its initialization before authoring
+external drafts. Schema evolution after the first authority advance is rejected.
+For an external draft, capture A, create the draft, edit it, then publish it using
+the same capture. An intervening authority advance makes that publication stale.
+If external acceptance has already occurred, reconciliation is required; do not
+silently recapture or reauthor the accepted attempt. Ordinary optimistic edits
+may continue concurrently.
+
+## What actually changes
+
+* `shared-tree-core/editManager.ts` retains original Fluid-reference checkpoints
+  **T**, plus separate approved **A**, sequenced provisional **P** and local **L**
+  history roles. Historical sequence mappings are not rewritten when A advances.
+* Peer reconstruction rebases a sender's pending branch onto its original T
+  checkpoint, not a new interpretation of the old reference.
+* An external draft extends A, or its explicitly accepted prefix. Existing rebasers
+  transform the remaining P over the new A, then
+  L over that new sequenced trunk. Live checkout observers receive the resulting
+  transition through the existing rebase event, not a silent `setHead`.
+* `sharedTreeCore.ts` wraps existing change codecs in one versioned authority
+  envelope. Draft changes retain revision/session/compressor context and are
+  enriched using their actual draft checkout. Fluid acknowledgment still retires
+  ordinary retransmission; authority approval retires P separately.
+* `externalAuthorityController.ts` provides capture, ordinary draft editing and
+  publication without exposing changeset internals or a second application queue.
+* Authority-mode undo resolves the original stable revision in the **current
+  semantic history** before inversion. Inverting the old retained authoring
+  commit instead would restore an obsolete pre-authority value. This opt-in
+  behavior is propagated to forks; normal-mode undo is unchanged.
+
+## Deliberate limits
+
+* **No runtime summaries.** Attach/bootstrap summaries use existing machinery;
+  runtime summaries after sequenced authority-mode work fail explicitly. Do not
+  enable production summarization or claim durable reopen/replay support.
+  Pre-attach seed edits become the approved baseline with P empty. Bootstrap
+  summaries persist all three configured authority labels; loading checks them
+  before loading tree state. Wrong/missing labels or loading an authority summary
+  with an ordinary factory fails explicitly. Older experimental summaries without
+  this metadata are deliberately unsupported, rather than silently reinterpreted.
+  Matching-label bootstrap load is tested using a separately deserialized
+  IdCompressor. This does not establish later authority-history recovery.
+* All history, removed-root retention and authority deduplication records are
+  retained without a bound. This is for bounded experiments, not production GC.
+* Reconnect/resubmit of ordinary work and an authority publication have dedicated
+  real-client tests. Persisted pending-state replay and summary reconstruction are
+  not thereby proved.
+* No non-prefix subset acceptance, revision-discarding transformed/coalesced
+  replacement, rejection rollback,
+  arbitrary historical base authoring, authority election, or cryptographic
+  authorization. Duplicate batch IDs with different content are protocol errors.
+* No shared branches or schema evolution. Required-field vacancy and required-field
+  reinsertion remain unsupported. Same-object sequence/optional reinsertion is an
+  additional explicit opt-in in this revision, not enabled by authority mode alone.
+* Existing move and removal/undo identity are tested across authority rebases.
+  That is not a substitute for general Detach/Attach.
+* Conflicting-write undo/redo is tested across repeated authority advances.
+  Accepted-prefix corrections are later authoritative edits, not rewritten
+  versions of accepted edits and not entries in the user's undo stack. Undo of
+  an accepted revision uses ordinary selective-undo semantics through later
+  corrections; it is not a grouped undo of the acceptance plus normalization.
+  The conflicting-field case is compared against an ordinary SharedTree history.
+  An accepted detach transaction can be undone/redone while retaining later
+  normalized node content and per-client identity. This does not establish undo
+  semantics for future settlement that discards or replaces revisions.
+
+## Optional detached-root editing
+
+Add `enableDetachedRootEditing: true` to the factory configuration on **every**
+participant. The resulting authority DDS type ends in
+`/detached-roots-v1/external-authority-v2`, distinct from the authority-only type.
+Use a fresh experimental container; do not reinterpret an existing association.
+
+Removed hydrated roots can be supplied to ordinary array `insertAt*` methods
+or assigned to optional fields. They retain identity on the originating and
+remote clients. Fields under removed nodes can be edited before reinsertion.
+This uses detached-root renames/attachments in real changesets, not JSON copies
+or delete-undo emulation.
+
+Only roots from the same live checkout are accepted. Duplicate roots in one
+insertion, fresh/detached mixtures, and required-field reinsertion fail explicitly.
+Reusing removed children inside newly constructed enclosing objects is not
+implemented by this port. This is therefore **not full parity** with every
+capability of the older experimental package.
+
+The port also fixes the public branch's v3 encoder rejecting detached-root edits
+without a field-owned detach location. Such root edits are encoded separately
+by v3; older codecs continue rejecting unrepresentable global root edits.
+
+`src/test/shared-tree/detachedReinsertion.spec.ts` checks independent clients,
+concurrent reattachments and edits, removed-node edits, stable object identity,
+and combined authority/P/L processing. The combined traces retain original P
+identities, rebase pending detach/attach over external history, approve the same
+P edits, undo against the new approved history, and decode delayed ordinary
+operations in their original sender context.
+
+## Validation
+
+From the Fluid workspace, using its existing pnpm toolchain:
+
+```powershell
+$env:JS_DEVMAIN_USE_SYSTEM_TOOLS = '1'
+pnpm --filter @fluidframework/tree run build:esnext
+pnpm --filter @fluidframework/tree run build:test:esm
+pnpm --filter @fluidframework/tree run test:mocha:esm -- --grep 'external authority'
+```
+
+The suite is `src/test/shared-tree/externalAuthority.spec.ts`. It uses actual
+independent SharedTree clients and compressors and compares tree content,
+observable atomic transitions and hydrated object identity. It includes the
+decisive order reversal (Fluid P writes 1, external A writes 2, rebased P still
+wins with 1), old-reference sender predecessors, local L, immutable cuts,
+prefix/dedup/stale handling, undo, reconnect/resubmit, mixed runtime batches,
+explicit summary rejection and unsupported editing boundaries.
+
+`src/test/shared-tree/atomicAuthoritySettlement.spec.ts` additionally covers
+prefix-based normalization, later P/L, original sender contexts, no-net
+obligations, removed roots, corrective detach/reattach, undo/redo ancestry,
+duplicates, reconnect, stale publication, invalid prefix/capture/base combinations
+and protocol-version fencing. These are real DDS instances using existing mock
+ordering infrastructure, **not AFR or OCS executions**.
